  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Spanish lyric-theatre tradition combining sung-operatic and spoken-dialogue sections, named after the Royal Palacio de la Zarzuela where the early seventeenth-century court entertainments were performed. The modern zarzuela emerged in mid-nineteenth-century Madrid through Francisco Asenjo Barbieri and Federico Chueca; the género chico shorter one-act form developed in 1880s-1900s and crystallized the canonical Madrid-urban-life subject matter. Distinguished from broader European operetta by zarzuela's Spanish-specific musical idioms (jota, habanera, fandango integration) and Madrid-and-regional Spanish settings.",
